terrarium
=========

Puts an installed Zainium OS on a USB drive, straight from the ISO.

The drive then starts any computer, UEFI or BIOS, and keeps your files and
apps, like an installed system. It can be encrypted.

    terrarium --list                     the USB drives on this computer
    terrarium <iso> <usb>                install Zainium OS on the drive
    terrarium --encrypt <iso> <usb>      the same, encrypted

It asks for your name, a user name and a password, then for the drive name
before it erases anything. It only writes to USB drives, never to the disk
the computer runs from. At the end it checks the drive once: the boot files,
the kernel and initramfs, and every file of the base.

It runs as root:

    elevate terrarium zainium-live-2026.11.13.iso /dev/sdc

It works on any Linux. The programs it needs (mkfs.ext4, e2image,
cryptsetup, limine) come from the ISO itself and run with the ISO's own
loader and libraries, so nothing has to be installed first. The computer's
kernel needs loop devices, EROFS and ext4 fs-verity, which current
distributions have.

terrarium uses the install engine of the Zainium installer
(github.com/Zainium-Dynamics/zainium-installer, pkg/), so the drive holds the
same system the installer makes.

Build
-----

    make
    make install DESTDIR=<root>

